# Articles Reference Content

When to reach for the definite article, and every form it takes.

## Purpose

Teach deployment, not formation. The six-slot paradigm is a lookup; the error learners actually make is omitting an article Greek requires, or supplying one it refuses.

## Key Insight

Forming the article is a lookup. Knowing when to use it is the skill. Greek puts an article in front of countries, abstract nouns, whole categories and possessives, where English refuses one.

Gender detection lives on the Nouns tab — the ending tells you the gender, the gender picks the column here.

---

## Usage vs English (Hero Element)

<insight name="article-usage" highlight="true">
<title>Greek adds it — English doesn't</title>

| Greek | English | Why |
|-------|---------|-----|
| η Ελλάδα | Greece | Countries, and first names in speech — ο Γιάννης |
| η αγάπη είναι τυφλή | love is blind | Abstract nouns |
| ο φίλος μου | my friend | Possessives keep the article — never "a friend of mine" |
| μου αρέσουν οι γάτες | I like cats | Whole categories, stated in the plural |
| το Σάββατο | on Saturday | Days and dates |

</insight>

<insight name="article-omission">
<title>Greek drops it — English keeps it</title>

| Greek | English | Why |
|-------|---------|-----|
| είναι γιατρός | he is a doctor | Professions after είμαι take no article at all |

</insight>

