## Article Quick Lookup (Expanded by Default)

<paradigm name="article-paradigm" default="expanded">

### Singular

|      | Masculine | Feminine | Neuter |
|------|-----------|----------|--------|
| Nom  | ο         | η        | το     |
| Acc  | τον       | τη(ν)    | το     |
| Gen  | του       | της      | του    |

### Plural

|      | Masculine | Feminine | Neuter |
|------|-----------|----------|--------|
| Nom  | οι        | οι       | τα     |
| Acc  | τους      | τις      | τα     |
| Gen  | των       | των      | των    |

</paradigm>

---

## Quick Win: The -ν Rule

<insight name="nu-rule" highlight="true">
<title>When uncertain, use the -ν form</title>
<explanation>
You'll never be wrong if you keep the final -ν on τον/την/στον/στην.
Native speakers sometimes drop it, but keeping it is never wrong.
</explanation>
<example>τον φίλο or τη φίλη - both correct!</example>
</insight>

---

## The -ν Rule (Movable Nu) - Detailed

<section name="movable-nu">
<title>Fine-tuning: when to keep or drop final -ν</title>

<rule>
Keep the -ν before vowels and the κ, π, τ family (ξ, ψ, γκ, μπ, ντ) - the hard
stops the ν blends into (την πόρτα flows out as "tim-bórta").
Drop the -ν before stretchable sounds (β, γ, δ, ζ, θ, λ, μ, ν, ρ, σ, φ, χ).

The rule is not article-specific: the negatives δεν and μην follow it too.
δεν πάω (before π) but δε θέλω (before θ).

Exception: masculine τον keeps its -ν everywhere (τον φίλο, never το φίλο) -
the -ν is all that separates it from neuter το. The sound rule governs
feminine τη(ν)/στη(ν) and the negatives.
</rule>

### Keep the -ν (minimal pairs with the drop column)
- την άνοιξη (before vowel)
- στην πόρτα (before π)
- δεν πάω (before π)

### Drop the -ν
- τη μητέρα (before μ)
- στη θάλασσα (before θ)
- δε θέλω (before θ)

</section>

---

## Article or Pronoun? (Shared with the Pronouns tab)

<insight name="article-or-pronoun" highlight="true">
<title>Article or pronoun?</title>
<explanation>
Eight article forms are spelled exactly like pronouns — the whole third person of the
pronoun system. The form never tells you which one you are reading. Its neighbour does:
a noun after it means article, a verb after it means object pronoun, and a noun before
it means possessive.
</explanation>

| Form | Before a noun ("the") | Before a verb (him/her/it) | After a noun (his/her/their) |
|------|-----------------------|----------------------------|------------------------------|
| τον  | τον φίλο              | τον ξέρω                   | —                            |
| την  | την πόρτα             | την ακούω                  | —                            |
| το   | το σπίτι              | το θέλω                    | —                            |
| τους | τους φίλους           | τους ξέρω                  | το σπίτι τους                |
| τις  | τις μέρες             | τις βλέπω                  | —                            |
| τα   | τα παιδιά             | τα βλέπω                   | —                            |
| του (m) | του φίλου          | του λέω                    | ο φίλος του                  |
| του (n) | του σπιτιού        | του δίνω γάλα              | το χρώμα του                 |
| της  | της Μαρίας            | της λέω                    | η μητέρα της                 |

</insight>

---

## Common Gender Mistakes (Collapsible)

<mistakes expandable="true" default="collapsed">

| Wrong              | Correct            | Why                                      |
|--------------------|--------------------|------------------------------------------|
| τα άνθρωπο         | τον άνθρωπο        | άνθρωπος is masculine, not neuter plural |
| ο μητέρα μου       | η μητέρα μου       | μητέρα is feminine, not masculine        |
| η βιβλίο           | το βιβλίο          | βιβλίο is neuter, not feminine           |

</mistakes>

